{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for exact head d4107613c1d7dfdd6fdebb41f2d9b3649202effe. Two security/trust-boundary defects remain. First, the public handle is an unkeyed deterministic hash of an unconstrained caller-chosen bearer secret, truncated to 64 bits. The service does not require high-entropy IDs: in an isolated running branch service I announced session `alice`; presence published `session:781a141d99b6b1c6`, exactly reproduced offline as SHA-256(domain || `alice`) truncated to 8 bytes. An observer can confirm dictionary guesses, then present the guessed raw ID. That guess authorized an effective durable operator act. MCP’s 96 random bits and the browser’s UUID do not make an API-wide assumption safe; mint an unrelated public handle server-side (or enforce and key an adequate construction), and avoid the needless 64-bit collision surface. Second, the new reference says durable acts are unaffected and nothing reachable over the interface can forge one. `/v0/act` does the opposite by design: it resolves the same session ID, selects that actor’s custodial key, and calls `Workspace.Act`; the isolated guessed-ID probe produced an effective operator-signed assert. State plainly that the loopback/session boundary authorizes durable acts as well as speech, and add a service-level test that a published handle cannot authorize `/v0/act`, not only nexus-level speech/departure tests. The intended non-disclosure property otherwise holds: live probes found no raw ID in announce, presence, wait/watch, frames, conversation lists, or errors; published handles failed as say/depart/act credentials; adapted existing tests retain their original assertions; make vet/test/race/build and diff --check pass; worktree clean. No merge performed.","body":{"finding_count":"2","head":"d4107613c1d7dfdd6fdebb41f2d9b3649202effe","verdict":"changes-requested"}}